    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d23">

                <pb facs="0088" n="72" xml:id="pg72"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/9–2650</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minute of Conversation Between the Secretary of State
                        and the British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bevin</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d23-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">New
                                York</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1950-09-26"
                            >September 26, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In a conversation on other matters, Mr. Bevin said that the British were very
                    much concerned about the Michael Scott Case.</p>
                <p>Mr. Acheson said that we were going into this problem very carefully and
                    mentioned the fact that the passage of the McCarran Bill somewhat complicated
                    our handling of the problem.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d23-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lucius</hi> D. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Battle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d23-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d23-f1-content">
                <p>Drafted by Lucius D. Battle of the Executive Secretariat.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f2" n="2" xml:id="d23-f2-content">
                <p>This refers to the Internal Security Act of 1950 (64 Stat. 987), which was
                    enacted into law by the Houses of Congress over the President’s objection on
                    September 23. Documentation on the impact of this legislation on the conduct of
                    United States foreign relations is scheduled for publication in <ref
                        target="frus1950v01">volume <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>
                    </ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
